A route-level loader (the pattern behind React Router's `loader` and similar APIs in other routers) kicks the fetch off as soon as the route is matched, in parallel with — not after — the page component being prepared to render:

```jsx
// Router config: the loader runs as soon as this route is matched,
// concurrently with loading the route's component code.
const routes = [
  {
    path: "/users/:id",
    loader: ({ params }) => fetchUser(params.id),
    Component: UserPage,
  },
];

function UserPage() {
  const user = useLoaderData();   // already resolved by the time this renders
  return <h1>{user.name}</h1>;
}
```

`fetchUser` starts the moment the router matches `/users/:id` — it doesn't wait for `UserPage` to mount and run an effect first. Compare this to a `useEffect`-based fetch inside `UserPage`, which can only start once the component has already rendered once with no data: the loader pattern removes an entire render-then-fetch round trip from the critical path.

```jsx
function ProfilePage({ userId }) {
  const [user, setUser] = useState(null);
  useEffect(() => { fetchUser(userId).then(setUser); }, [userId]);

  if (!user) return <Spinner />;
  return <PostsList userId={user.id} />;   // only mounts once user exists
}

function PostsList({ userId }) {
  const [posts, setPosts] = useState(null);
  useEffect(() => { fetchPosts(userId).then(setPosts); }, [userId]);
  // ...
}
```

The waterfall here isn't obvious from reading either component in isolation — each looks like a perfectly ordinary fetch-in-an-effect. The problem is structural: `PostsList` doesn't even **mount** until `user` exists, so its effect (and therefore `fetchPosts`) can't start until `fetchUser` has already fully resolved — even though `fetchPosts` only needs `userId`, which was known immediately, before `fetchUser` even started.

The fix is to lift both fetches to a common point that has `userId` immediately — a route loader, or a parent effect that kicks off both requests together with `Promise.all`-style concurrency — so `fetchPosts` starts at the same time as `fetchUser` instead of waiting for a value (`user.id`) it never actually needed in the first place.
